WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.989
A thirteen-pound iron bar.

00:00:01.999 --> 00:00:03.044
Through his head.

00:00:03.054 --> 00:00:05.610
1848, Vermont.

00:00:05.635 --> 00:00:08.088
Railroad foreman
Phineas Gage was setting a

00:00:08.098 --> 00:00:10.399
blast when the powder went off early.

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:11.955
The rod shot up through his left

00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:14.305
cheek, behind his eye, and out the

00:00:14.315 --> 00:00:15.634
top of his skull.

00:00:15.644 --> 00:00:17.395
It destroyed much of his left frontal

00:00:17.405 --> 00:00:18.245
lobe.

00:00:18.255 --> 00:00:19.408
He survived.

00:00:19.418 --> 00:00:21.258
The famous story says it turned him

00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:22.644
into a different man.

00:00:22.654 --> 00:00:25.808
Historians say it was badly exaggerated:

00:00:25.821 --> 00:00:28.336
the change was real, but faded within

00:00:28.346 --> 00:00:29.648
a few years.

00:00:29.658 --> 00:00:31.773
And photos of him afterwards have turned

00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:32.399
up.

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:33.997
So what did Gage do next?

00:00:34.007 --> 00:00:35.812
He moved to Chile and drove a

00:00:35.822 --> 00:00:39.400
stagecoach, handling six
horses and passengers who

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:40.759
didn't speak English.

00:00:40.769 --> 00:00:42.709
Researchers now think he recovered a great

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:43.569
deal.

00:00:43.582 --> 00:00:46.469
Yet most textbooks still leave Chile out.
